The CSS corpus: 15 real, high-traffic pages, captured verbatim.

WHAT IT IS FOR
This is the WORK ORDER for the CSS engine. `make audit-css` runs the real
pipeline (dom_parse -> css_expand_vars -> css_apply -> css_extra_apply ->
layout_page) over these bytes with a reporter installed inside LibCSS's
parseProperty(), and ranks every declaration the cascade throws away and every
layout mode the pages need. What gets implemented is decided by counting what
these pages actually declare -- not by reading a spec, and never by counting
supported properties.

That distinction is not pedantry. This project has a documented case of an HTML
conformance score going from 2/1643 to 1723/1818 while the browser still could
not run one real site's JavaScript. A property count can rise while pages get
no better; a page either lays out or it does not.

WHY THEY ARE COMMITTED RATHER THAN FETCHED
Same reason as tests/fixtures/cssperf: a corpus fetched at measure time changes
under the measurement. These are the exact bytes the before/after numbers in
the commit messages were measured on, and `make audit-css-before` re-measures
them against a build with the changes disabled. Refetching invalidates both
columns at once, so the numbers must be re-taken if anyone refreshes these.

It is ~14 MB of HTML and CSS, which is not free. It buys the ability to say
"tailwind.com went from 0 flex containers to 151" and have that be a fact about
a specific artefact rather than about whatever the CDN served that afternoon.

WHY THESE FIFTEEN -- the spread is the point
  news        apnews, bbc          bbc is styled ENTIRELY from inline <style>
  docs        mdn, pydocs, web-dev mdn is grid-heavy; pydocs is classic block flow
  search      bing, ddg            server-rendered result lists
  product     apple, tailwind      apple is var()-heavy; tailwind is @layer-only
  app UI      github               Primer: 283 flex containers over 1998 elements
  CN portal   163, baidu, qq, sina float-era layout, CJK text, few flex
  control     hn                   a table-based page from 2007, no flex at all

hn and pydocs are there deliberately as CONTROLS: a change that helps the
modern pages must not move these, and if it does, something is being applied
that the page never asked for.

CAPTURE
  ./capture.py <name> <url>
fetches the page and every <link rel=stylesheet> it names (up to 26), writing
index.html, sheet-N.css and a MANIFEST recording each source URL. It ignores
`data-href` -- GitHub ships twelve inert theme <link>s that way and counting
them would triple the corpus's CSS without adding a rule that ever applies.

Captured 2026-08-08, unmodified. These are third-party pages kept verbatim as
test input only; each remains the copyright of its publisher. MANIFEST in each
directory records exactly where every byte came from.
